  "title": "Gmail API Introduces Granular OAuth Scopes",
  "summary": "The Gmail API has rolled out a comprehensive set of new, granular OAuth2 scopes, enabling developers to request more precise permissions for drafts, messages, labels, and filters. This update aims to enhance security and user privacy by promoting the principle of least privilege.",
  "details": "Developers can now utilize fine-grained permissions such as `https://www.googleapis.com/auth/gmail.drafts.create` for composing drafts, `https://www.googleapis.com/auth/gmail.messages.readonly` for reading emails, `https://www.googleapis.com/auth/gmail.labels.create` for label management, and `https://www.googleapis.com/auth/gmail.settings.filters.readonly` for filter access. Numerous methods across `users.drafts`, `users.messages`, `users.labels`, `users.threads`, and `users.settings.filters` have been updated to list these new, specific scopes as primary options. While broader scopes like `gmail.modify` and `gmail.readonly` are still supported, developers are strongly encouraged to review their applications and migrate to these more targeted scopes to improve security posture and user trust.",
